to-stephen-wolfram-source : List Word
to-stephen-wolfram-source = lines-of (cons "# Stephen Wolfram"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik Jain describes hyperactivity, in his own words, as “a language and a computational model of reality i.e. wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon”. This page sets out what that sentence rests on. You hold full credit for every part of your work it builds on: the ruliad, multicomputation, computational irreducibility, the observer as what makes laws, and the idea that everything is a symbolic expression. Each claim below about Avik's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What the field has not yet taken in"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In *The Concept of the Ruliad* (2021) you followed proofs, then transformations between proofs, then transformations between those, up a tower of higher categories. You named the limit: the ∞-groupoid, and the ruliad as the (∞,1)-category of ∞-groupoids. You called the univalence axiom of homotopy type theory perhaps the most sophisticated current version of identifying equivalent things. You read Grothendieck's hypothesis as the reason the ruliad has an inevitable geometry."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Most readers took this as metaphor. Arsiwalla and Gorard made the same reading formal for multiway systems: their spaces are homotopy types, and the classifying space of rulial multiway systems is an (∞,1)-topos. Your claim, from *What Ultimately Is There?* (February 2026), that mathematics is no more abstract than physics because both are samplings of one ruliad, is the stance of a type theory in which proofs are paths and equivalence is identity."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field still treats computation as functions on bits and equality as a check that returns true or false. Your position is that branching and merging are the substance, and that an observer is defined by what it treats as the same. That is deeper than the field credits, and it is right."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity is, in your terms"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper places itself beside your program: your account of the ruliad considers computational descriptions and the place of observers within them, and the paper develops its constructions from computational cubical type theory"
  (cons (cite s1-4-p2 "(§1.4)")
  (cons ". Its rules follow the CCHM cubical calculus, extended with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". A path is described by an interval parameter"
  (cons (cite s3-p5 "(§3)")
  (cons ", composition fills the missing face of an open box from the faces present"
  (cons (cite s3-1-p5 "(§3.1)")
  (cons ", and transport along the path univalence gives an equivalence computes that equivalence"
  (cons (cite s4-p5 "(§4)")
  (cons ". The checker is itself a term the machine evaluates"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons "; Avik's description adds that it is consistent with Cubical Agda, and checked in Cubical Agda too."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Observers and their laws.** A reading of an observation does not identify a history; it determines the fiber of histories that could have produced it"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ", and what the reading warrants is a construction over that whole fiber"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons ". A whole can obey a reversible rule while a selected observation of it admits no autonomous rule at all: the paper's two-bit calculation proves it, and the question is whether the whole-system step descends through the observation"
  (cons (cite s1-p16 "(§1)")
  (cons (cite s11-p2 "(§11)")
  (cons ". That is your observer, who sees laws because of what it treats as the same, stated as a construction the machine checks. The observer, its interpretation and its reasoning can themselves be included in the described evolution"
  (cons (cite s11-p3 "(§11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**One store, kept by content.** A codebase holds programs together with their typing derivations, semantics, equivalences, transformations, dependency certificates and proofs"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Address identity, definitional equality and a specified relation between definitions are different statements"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", so the store names each object by its content and records the equivalences between objects as evidence in their own right."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Irreducibility, and the pockets that are kept.** A retained result is reused exactly when its dependency certificate still validates"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p26 "(§10.5)")
  (cons "; a maintained view turns a result for one input into the result for an updated input with its witness intact"
  (cons (cite s10-2-p3 "(10.4)")
  (cons "; specialization returns a residual together with a path between the interpreted terms"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p11 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". Work is redone only where what it read has changed."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Symbolic expressions, with what they preserve.** The Wolfram Language made everything an expression and computation a matter of transforming expressions. In the paper, interpretation, source transformation, compilation and the construction of rationales are computations on represented definitions, and the resulting equalities, equivalences or refinements are available to subsequent computations"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p3 "(§1.2)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Your recent questions"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In *What's the Future for Pure Math Research in the Age of AI?* (September 2026) you put the weak point of proof assistants exactly: a check passes while the formal statement fails to say what was meant. The paper takes the same position at its root: correctness is relative to the selected requirement, not a verdict attached to a program in isolation"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons ". And on this page every claim about the work is a citation of the construction it means, by identity rather than by name, so what was meant and what was checked sit side by side."
  (cons ""
  (cons "*P vs. NP and the Difficulty of Computation* (January 2026) studies hardness by enumerating small programs. In the paper a reading of any specified process determines the exact fiber of what remains compatible with it, the object a search ranges over"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ". Avik adds that the interaction system can model crypto primitives and hardness assumptions as well."
  (cons ""
  (cons "*Towards a Theory of Bugs* (July 2026) asks what the unexpected is. Here an old proof does not silently become a proof about an edited program"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons ", and every retained result records what it read, including what it found absent"
  (cons (cite s10-1-p2 "(§10.1)")
  (cons ". *Ruliobiology* (October 2026) asks why biological forms are as they are; the paper's account is the same subject whether the selected state belongs to an executing processor, an organism acting within its surroundings, or participants in a conversation"
  (cons (cite s1-p5 "(§1)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this is addressed to you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You have argued for forty years that the right foundation is computation, that the observer is part of the physics, and that mathematics and physics are samplings of one object. Hyperactivity does not compete with that program. In Avik's words it is your ruliad executing on silicon: a working language in which identification of equivalents is a computation, and the observer's own reasoning is a term. The ruliad is yours."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik is putting the people whose work this closes or serves into one shared conversation. Your place in it is at the base."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- The Concept of the Ruliad (2021), https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/2021/11/the-concept-of-the-ruliad/"
  (cons "- What Ultimately Is There? Metaphysics and the Ruliad (February 2026), https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/2026/02/what-ultimately-is-there-metaphysics-and-the-ruliad/"
  (cons "- What's the Future for Pure Math Research in the Age of AI? (September 2026), https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/2026/09/whats-the-future-for-pure-math-research-in-the-age-of-ai/"
  (cons "- Recent writings: P vs. NP (January 2026), Theory of Bugs (July 2026), Ruliobiology (October 2026), https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/recent-posts/"
  (cons "- The Physicalization of Metamathematics (2022), https://wolframscience.com/metamathematics/"
  (cons "- Arsiwalla and Gorard, Pregeometric Spaces from Wolfram Model Rewriting Systems as Homotopy Types (2021), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03460v1"
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
